      "keyInsight": "Here is a bizarre modern mystery: ancient skulls from thousands of years ago almost always had straight, perfect teeth and massive, wide jaws. Nobody back then had braces, retainers, or wisdom tooth surgery. Today, nearly everyone has crooked teeth and crowded airways. Did our genetics suddenly fail us over a few centuries? Not at all. The real culprit is sitting on your dinner plate: industrial soft food. Our ancestors spent hours chewing tough roots, raw plants, and fibrous meats. That intense chewing acted like daily weightlifting for the jawbones, pulling them outward and building roomy airways behind them. When we invented processed mush, white flour, and canned soup, our jaws stopped working out. Without physical mechanical stress, our mouth bones shrank across generations. Our teeth remained the exact same size, so they had nowhere to go except sideways and crooked. Even worse, the smaller jaw pushed our tongue back into our throat, shrinking the tunnel we breathe through. You do not just chew to digest food. You chew to keep the highway to your lungs open.",
